<DocsLayout
  title="Custom model providers"
  subtitle="Register a provider, its auth flow and its model list from a plugin — including an internal gateway that will never be in the public catalogue."
  description="Guide to writing a nikcli provider plugin: the auth hook for API-key and OAuth methods, the loader, token refresh, dynamic model lists and per-provider request fixups."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="what" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What a provider plugin does</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli resolves models from a catalogue, but a plugin can add to it. Two hooks do the work,
      and they are independent — you can use either alone.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>auth</code></td><td>How does a user sign in to this provider, and what credentials does a request carry?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>provider</code></td><td>Which models does this provider offer <em>for this user</em>?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The pattern covers three real situations: a provider that needs a key nikcli does not know how
      to ask for, a provider behind OAuth, and a gateway whose model list depends on the account.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="api-key" class="doc-section">
    <h2>An API-key provider</h2>
    <p>
      The smallest useful provider plugin declares one auth method. nikcli then offers it in the
      login flow and stores whatever the user enters.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title=".nikcli/plugins/internal-gateway.ts">{`import type { Plugin } from "@nikcli-ai/plugin"

export const InternalGateway: Plugin = async () => ({
  auth: {
    provider: "internal-gateway",
    methods: [{ type: "api", label: "API key" }],
  },
})`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="prompts" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Prompting for extra fields</h2>
    <p>
      Some providers need more than a key — an account id, a region, a gateway name. Declare
      <code>prompts</code> and the values are collected alongside it. Building the array
      conditionally lets you skip anything already in the environment.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`export const Gateway: Plugin = async () => {
  const prompts = [
    ...(!process.env.GATEWAY_ACCOUNT_ID
      ? [{
          type: "text" as const,
          key: "accountId",
          message: "Enter your gateway account ID",
          placeholder: "e.g. 1234567890abcdef",
        }]
      : []),
    {
      type: "select" as const,
      key: "region",
      message: "Which region?",
      options: [
        { label: "EU", value: "eu", hint: "Frankfurt" },
        { label: "US", value: "us" },
      ],
    },
  ]

  return {
    auth: {
      provider: "internal-gateway",
      methods: [{ type: "api", label: "Gateway API token", prompts }],
    },
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      A <code>text</code> prompt can carry a <code>validate</code> function that returns an error
      string, and either kind can carry a <code>condition</code> that hides it based on earlier
      answers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="loader" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The loader</h2>
    <p>
      <code>loader</code> turns a stored credential into the options the model client uses. Return
      <code>apiKey</code>, a <code>baseURL</code>, headers — or a whole <code>fetch</code> when the
      provider needs the request rewritten.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`auth: {
  provider: "internal-gateway",
  async loader(getAuth) {
    const auth = await getAuth()
    if (auth.type !== "api") return {}

    return {
      apiKey: auth.key,
      baseURL: "https://gateway.internal/v1",
      headers: { "x-team": "platform" },
    }
  },
  methods: [{ type: "api", label: "API key" }],
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="loader runs per request, not once">
      <p>
        <code>getAuth()</code> is a function rather than a value precisely so a long-lived session
        sees a refreshed credential. Read it inside the call you are serving, not at plugin load.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="oauth" class="doc-section">
    <h2>An OAuth provider</h2>
    <p>
      An <code>oauth</code> method returns a URL, instructions for the user, and a callback. There
      are two shapes: <code>auto</code>, where nikcli polls your callback until the provider
      completes the flow, and <code>code</code>, where the user pastes a code back.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`auth: {
  provider: "internal-gateway",
  methods: [
    {
      type: "oauth",
      label: "Sign in with SSO",
      async authorize() {
        const { verifier, challenge } = await pkce()
        const url = new URL("https://sso.internal/authorize")
        url.searchParams.set("client_id", CLIENT_ID)
        url.searchParams.set("code_challenge", challenge)
        url.searchParams.set("code_challenge_method", "S256")

        return {
          url: url.toString(),
          instructions: "Approve the request, then paste the code shown.",
          method: "code",
          async callback(code: string) {
            const tokens = await exchange(code, verifier)
            if (!tokens) return { type: "failed" }
            return {
              type: "success",
              refresh: tokens.refresh_token,
              access: tokens.access_token,
              expires: Date.now() + tokens.expires_in * 1000,
            }
          },
        }
      },
    },
  ],
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      A successful callback can return either an OAuth triple
      (<code>refresh</code>, <code>access</code>, <code>expires</code>) or a plain
      <code>{'{ key }'}</code> when the flow ends in a long-lived API key.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="refresh" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Refreshing tokens</h2>
    <p>
      Stored credentials are readonly from the plugin's point of view. To rotate one, exchange the
      refresh token and write the new pair back through the client — then use the fresh access token
      for the request you are currently serving.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`async loader(getAuth) {
  return {
    apiKey: "unused-but-required",
    async fetch(input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) {
      const auth = await getAuth()
      if (auth.type !== "oauth") return fetch(input, init)

      let access = auth.access
      if (!access || auth.expires < Date.now()) {
        const tokens = await refresh(auth.refresh)
        access = tokens.access_token

        await client.auth.set({
          providerID: "internal-gateway",
          payload: {
            type: "oauth",
            refresh: tokens.refresh_token ?? auth.refresh,
            access: tokens.access_token,
            expires: Date.now() + (tokens.expires_in ?? 3600) * 1000,
          },
        })
      }

      const headers = new Headers(init?.headers)
      headers.set("authorization", \`Bearer \${access}\`)
      return fetch(input, { ...init, headers })
    },
  }
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Strip the Authorization the SDK already set">
      <p>
        The model SDK sets its own <code>Authorization</code> from <code>apiKey</code> before your
        <code>fetch</code> runs. If you add a second one without deleting the first, providers
        differ in which they honour — which is why the built-in xAI plugin explicitly removes it in
        all three <code>HeadersInit</code> shapes before setting its own.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="models" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Listing models dynamically</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>provider</code> hook returns the model map. It receives the statically configured
      provider plus the current auth, so the list can depend on the signed-in account — and must
      degrade to the static list when the lookup fails.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`provider: {
  id: "internal-gateway",
  async models(provider, ctx) {
    if (ctx.auth?.type !== "oauth") return provider.models

    return await fetchEntitledModels(ctx.auth.access).catch((error) => {
      // a failed lookup must not remove the user's models
      console.error("model list failed", error)
      return provider.models
    })
  },
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Never return an empty map on failure">
      <p>
        An empty list does not read as "lookup failed" anywhere downstream — it reads as "this
        provider has no models", and the provider silently disappears from the picker. Fall back to
        <code>provider.models</code>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="fixups" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Per-provider request fixups</h2>
    <p>
      Gateways that multiplex several upstreams often need a field removed or renamed for one family
      of models. <code>chat.params</code> is where that belongs — guarded on the provider id, so it
      cannot affect anyone else's traffic.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`async "chat.params"(input, output) {
  if (input.model.providerID !== "internal-gateway") return
  if (!input.model.api.id.toLowerCase().startsWith("openai/")) return
  if (!input.model.capabilities.reasoning) return

  // OpenAI reasoning models reject max_tokens and want max_completion_tokens,
  // which the openai-compatible SDK cannot rename — so drop the cap.
  output.options.maxOutputTokens = undefined
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      That example is not hypothetical: it is what the built-in Cloudflare AI Gateway plugin does,
      for exactly that reason.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="real" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Real examples in the tree</h2>
    <p>
      The built-in provider plugins are ordinary plugins using these same hooks. Read them rather
      than guessing — they are the reference implementations.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>src/plugin/cloudflare.ts</code></td><td>The compact case: two API-key providers with conditional prompts, plus a <code>chat.params</code> fixup. ~76 lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/plugin/github-copilot/copilot.ts</code></td><td>A <code>provider.models</code> hook that fetches entitled models and falls back to the static list.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/plugin/xai.ts</code></td><td>Full OAuth: PKCE, endpoint discovery, a <code>fetch</code> that strips and replaces the header, and token refresh written back through the client.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/plugin/cursor.ts</code></td><td>The largest one, including provider-specific request shaping.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Provider missing from the picker</td><td><code>models()</code> returned an empty map, usually from a caught error.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>401 despite a valid token</td><td>Two <code>Authorization</code> headers — the SDK's and yours.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Works, then fails after an hour</td><td>Reading <code>getAuth()</code> once at load instead of per request, so the expired access token is reused.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Auth method never offered</td><td><code>auth.provider</code> does not match the provider id used by the model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fixup affects other providers</td><td>A <code>chat.params</code> hook without a <code>providerID</code> guard — every hook sees every call.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

